<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Okinawa Reversion Agreement</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1971">1971</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Okinawa Reversion Agreement was a treaty between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, signed on 17 June 1971, under which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a>, administered by the United States since 1945, returned to Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> on 15 May 1972. The United States kept its military bases on Okinawa under the Japan–US security treaty, and the agreement became controversial for secret arrangements on nuclear weapons and costs.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> held its rights over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryukyu-islands">Ryukyu Islands</a> under Article III of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-san-francisco">Treaty of San Francisco</a>, which followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a>, and kept a large military presence on Okinawa, known during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> as the Keystone of the Pacific. Negotiations began in 1968 between the US Ambassador to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, U. Alexis Johnson, and the Japanese Foreign Minister, Kiichi Aichi. Japan wanted a firm date for reversion first; Aichi proposed 1972, suggesting that the bases keep their freedoms until both governments agreed on a gradual removal.</p><p>On 1 April 1969 the special envoy Kishi told President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-nixon">Richard Nixon</a> that many Japanese found it unacceptable for part of their country to remain occupied by a foreign power. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> made reversion conditional on nuclear weapons being allowed on Okinawa in an emergency, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> accepted, giving up its aim of complete denuclearisation. The talks also became tied to trade, with the United States pressing for limits on Japanese wool textiles.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The agreement, in nine major articles, was signed simultaneously in Washington and Tokyo by William P. Rogers for Nixon and Kiichi Aichi for Prime Minister Eisaku Satō. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> gave up its rights over the Ryukyu and Daitō Islands, which became subject to all treaties between the two countries, while its forces kept the use of facilities on Okinawa. The United States would help repair damage to land its administration had seized, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> recognised that administration's acts and agreed it would not be held liable for criminal activity, and Japan was to pay $320,000,000 over five years.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The National Diet ratified the agreement on 24 November 1971. It was controversial in Okinawa and on the Japanese mainland, mostly over the continued American military presence rather than reversion itself; in Tokyo radical students rioted with Molotov cocktails and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a> pipes and killed a police officer. Both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> criticised the agreement because the Senkaku Islands, which they claim, were included among the islands returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “1971 Okinawa Reversion Agreement” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Anger erupted in the Koza riot of 1970, and under the <b>Okinawa Reversion Agreement</b> the remaining islands returned to Japan on 15 May 1972, though major American bases remained.</blockquote><a href="/state/united-states-civil-administration-of-the-ryukyu-islands">United States Civil Administration of the Ryukyu Islands</a></li></ul></section>
